{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head 7b5c0701883a4062691a14afe7dd7d0141b3b14b, artifact 5f28945fa078b5c25227d924fa73e0e3bc3ceab2.\n\nThe gap was worse than the request described, in a way worth stating. docs/reference/gs/merge.md was not only missing the procedure — it contradicted the contract. Its Afterwards section still said to record the merge artifact \"superseding the previous artifact for the same path\", singular, which is precisely the rule the dot-artifact work replaced. So a reader following the reference would have done the wrong thing while believing they were following the manual. That is corrected, and it is the most valuable part of this change.\n\nThe re-cutting was the work rather than a copy, as I said in the promise. The reference now carries the rules in the form a reference reader wants: retiring and publishing as two decisions, the four cases as a table of situation and action, exact-string matching with the reason a near miss is silent rather than an error, the bare-supersede authority rule, and why nothing is published at `.`. The how-to carries the walkthrough at the point a reader meets it, between the merge and the requester's ratification — ask git which paths the merge changed, ask `gs status` which exact strings the live artifacts already use, then retire and publish — and links to the reference for the cases rather than restating them.\n\nThe example gate earned its keep. My first draft wrote `gs supersede --repo ... --as bot \"$ARTIFACT\" --text '...'`, and the docset example execution failed with \"supersede requires one target event\": Go stops flag parsing at the first positional, so the trailing `--text` was read as a second positional. A block that would have been copied by a reader and refused. The target now comes last and the gate passes.\n\nTwo anchoring problems found, neither fixed here, and I want to be explicit that this is a deliberate omission rather than an oversight.\n\nmerge.md rests on artifact 7bf40860, which is stale in the live workroom. This is the same stale citation I recorded at f0a5d254 against 3842d0a9, and it is unfixable without answering the question recorded there: 7bf40860 covers cmd/gs/main.go, and three artifacts for that path are live and non-stale, all pointing at branch heads. There is no single successor to choose, and picking one silently inside a documentation change is exactly what I argued against earlier today.\n\nend-to-end.md is anchored on comma-joined pseudo-paths — `AGENTS.md,notes/2026-08-07-bootstrap-task-cycle.md`, `spike/cmd/gs,docs`, and `spike/cmd/gs,docs/getting-started.md,docs/reference.md`. All three are live and non-stale, and they are live for the reason the guidance I just wrote gives: a comma-joined string is a path no real predecessor or successor can ever equal, so nothing can supersede it and it can never flare. The page teaching the rule is anchored on three violations of it. They cannot be repaired by editing frontmatter either — the artifacts themselves would need retiring and republishing at real paths, which is durable work by their authors.\n\nI would rather these be their own request than a rider here, and I will file one if the reviewer agrees that is the right shape.\n\nGates: `go test ./internal/docset -count=1` passes, including the anchor, flare, basis and example-execution gates. `go vet ./...` is clean. The worktree is clean at the reported head, which merges cleanly with main.","body":{"head":"7b5c0701883a4062691a14afe7dd7d0141b3b14b","status":"ready-for-review"}}